<!--
  aihu-goldeneye — presentational styled recipe (performativeUI port,
  Slice 11 — docs/plans/2026-08-01-performative-ui-port.md §Tier-C tail).
  "Isolated deliberately so it can be cut without blocking anything else" per
  the roadmap note; this is the last Tier-C decorative recipe in the batch.

  Design and behavior transcribed in spirit from performativeUI (MIT); no
  source text or `pui-` class names vendored — see NOTICES.md. The upstream
  demo file was neither fetched nor read for this port (no network access,
  and this port's binding provenance rule forbids transcribing upstream
  source on sight regardless): the effect below is written from the
  behavioral description in the slice brief plus ordinary, widely-known
  AI/tech marketing-site visual-effect conventions.

  ── Interpreting "goldeneye" ────────────────────────────────────────────

  The brief named three plausible readings for a component with this name in
  an AI-marketing-UI catalog: a Matrix-style digital-rain cascade, a
  scanning/targeting-reticle sweep, or a single glowing eye/orb. This recipe
  picks the third and treats it as the correct reading, not a coin flip:

    - The name decomposes literally as "gold" + "eye" — an eye, in a golden
      hue, not a rain of glyphs or a crosshair. Digital rain is also already
      this batch's `ascii-hero` territory (binary drift on a character
      field); a second glyph-cascade recipe this late in Tier-C would be a
      near-duplicate, not a new visual.
    - A single watchful eye is a genuinely common personification in
      AI-product marketing — "the system is perceiving/attending to you" —
      distinct from anything else in this batch (`floating-sparkles` is
      ambient texture, `node-graph-background` is a network metaphor,
      `ascii-hero` is a hero backdrop). It is also the reading that most
      naturally SUBSUMES the scanning-reticle idea rather than competing
      with it: this eye's pupil autonomously sweeps a slow scan path (see
      below), which reads as "scanning" without needing a separate
      crosshair motif.
    - The sweep is autonomous (a fixed Lissajous path driven by elapsed
      time), deliberately NOT pointer-tracking. A tracking eye would need a
      pointer listener and would blur the line into "interactive," but the
      brief is explicit that this is a decorative recipe with no
      interactive/keyboard/form/overlay capabilities (mirrored exactly in
      `meta.json`) — so "watchful" is expressed as a self-directed scan, not
      as attention paid to the visitor's cursor.

  ── Architecture: built directly on `useCanvasSurface` ──────────────────

  Neither `useParticleField` nor `useCharacterField` is the right fit here,
  and for a DIFFERENT reason than `node-graph-background`'s (Slice 10) case
  for dropping to the same level — that one needed a second draw pass over
  shared state and hit a missing draw hook. This recipe has no such
  workaround to make because it was never a fit for the "N-item field"
  abstraction those composables model in the first place:

    - `useParticleField` and `useCharacterField` both own a SEEDED ARRAY of
      many independent items (particles / glyph cells), each with its own
      position and (for particles) velocity, that must be seeded on first
      size, integrated every frame, and rescaled or rebuilt on resize. That
      machinery is the point of those composables.
    - This eye draws exactly ONE continuously-shaded feature — a radial
      glow, a solid iris disc, one sweeping pupil, one glint — whose
      geometry is a pure function of `(elapsed time, host width, host
      height)`, recomputed from scratch every frame. There is no seeded
      state to own, so there is also no `onResize` hook below: unlike every
      other Tier-C recipe in this port, a resize needs no rescale pass
      because nothing persists between frames to rescale.

  `useCanvasSurface` is explicitly "the substrate every canvas-based visual
  effect sits on … future canvas effects are expected to [sit on it] as
  well" (its own header), so entering at this level is the sanctioned
  choice for a custom single-feature draw, not a bypass — same verdict
  `node-graph-background` reached, arrived at independently here because
  this recipe's shape never matched a field composable's grain to begin
  with.

  ── The `globalAlpha` + `'transparent'`-stop idiom ──────────────────────

  The glow halo needs to fade from a caller-chosen `color` at some alpha
  down to nothing at its edge — but `color` is an arbitrary literal CSS
  string (hex/rgb/hsl/named; see below), and computing an alpha-adjusted
  variant of an unknown format would mean parsing it. Two things sidestep
  that entirely: `'transparent'` is a valid gradient color-stop regardless
  of the OTHER stop's format, so the far stop needs no relation to `color`
  at all; and the glow's overall peak brightness is carried by
  `ctx.globalAlpha` (reset to `1` after, same discipline `node-graph-background`
  and `useParticleField`'s `draw()` already follow), not by a baked-in alpha
  channel on the gradient itself. The iris disc, for the same reason, is
  drawn as a FLAT fill in `color` rather than a light-core/dark-rim
  gradient — a rim shade would need a lightened or darkened variant of an
  unparsed color string, which is exactly the constraint this idiom avoids.

  ── Props ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  color:       the iris and glow color. Default "#ffd700" (gold — the
               component's namesake hue). A LITERAL CSS color string, not a
               `--color-*` token reference — identical constraint to every
               other canvas recipe in this port (`floating-sparkles`,
               `node-graph-background`, `ascii-hero`): a canvas 2D context's
               `fillStyle` is not part of the CSS cascade and cannot resolve
               a custom property.
  pupil-color: the pupil's fill color. Default "#1c1408" (a near-black warm
               brown, legible against the default gold without reading as
               pure black-on-yellow). Also a literal CSS color string.
               (Declared `pupilColor`; the runtime derives the observed
               attribute as kebab-case, so consumers write `pupil-color`.)
  speed:       sweep/pulse rate multiplier, string-encoded number. Default
               "1". "0" freezes the sweep and glow at their `t=0` pose
               (still painted — see Reduced motion below — not blanked);
               this is a legitimate use, not an error case, so it is not
               coerced back up to a nonzero default.

  Not exposed: individual radii, glow strength, sweep amplitude, glint
  opacity. Every one of them is an internal look-and-feel constant at this
  altitude — widening the surface ahead of a real need is the
  over-engineering `ascii-hero`'s header already declined for this port.

  ── Layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A standalone, full-bleed, absolutely-positioned decorative layer
  (`position: absolute; inset: 0`), no slot — the same "decorative
  background layer" contract every Tier-C recipe in this port carries. The
  eye's own geometry is derived from the HOST's box (`Math.min(width,
  height)` sets the iris radius, `width/2, height/2` its center), so unlike
  a texture-fill recipe this also composes naturally as a compact element:
  a consumer can either drop it as the first child of a large
  `position: relative` hero (an ambient background eye) or size the host to
  a small square/circle wrapper for a compact "eye" placement — both are
  the same recipe, just a differently-sized host.

  ── Reduced motion / off-screen ─────────────────────────────────────────

  Neither is reimplemented here; both are `useCanvasSurface`'s job. Every
  animated term in `draw()` — the glow's pulse and both axes of the pupil's
  sweep — is a bare `Math.sin(t * frequency * TAU)` with NO phase offset, so
  at `t = 0` every one of them evaluates to exactly `0`: the pupil sits
  precisely centered in the iris and the glow sits at its baseline (not
  peak, not trough) brightness. `handleFrame` below maps
  `frame.reducedMotion` to `t = 0` for exactly this reason. Traced through
  `useCanvasSurface`'s "paint once, never loop" contract: under
  `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `start()` invokes `onFrame` exactly once
  with `reducedMotion: true` and never arms the rAF loop, so `handleFrame`
  computes `t = 0`, `draw()` renders that centered-pupil / baseline-glow
  frame, and nothing further is painted — a settled, watchful eye at rest,
  never a blank box and never frozen mid-sweep. `isVisible`-gated pausing
  while scrolled off-screen is inherited for free.

  `shadow: 'light'` — see the parent execution plan's theming decision.
-->

@state {
  import { cn } from '@aihu/css-engine/runtime/cn'

  shadow: 'light'

  let color = prop({ default: '#ffd700' })
  let pupilColor = prop({ default: '#1c1408' })
  let speed = prop({ default: '1' })

  let hostEl = state(null)

  // Read every prop into a differently-named local before use — an
  // options-object key textually identical to a prop name trips the
  // compiler's prop-auto-unwrap rewriter (see token-stream.aihu's header for
  // the exact failure mode).
  const irisColor = color
  const pupilColorValue = pupilColor
  const rateMultiplier = Math.max(0, Number(speed) || 0)

  const TAU = Math.PI * 2
  /** Iris radius as a fraction of the host's shorter side. */
  const IRIS_RADIUS_RATIO = 0.32
  /** Glow halo radius as a multiple of the iris radius. */
  const GLOW_RADIUS_RATIO = 2.2
  /** Glow's breathing alpha ranges [BASE - AMPLITUDE, BASE + AMPLITUDE]. */
  const GLOW_ALPHA_BASE = 0.28
  const GLOW_ALPHA_AMPLITUDE = 0.18
  /** Cycles per second (pre-`rateMultiplier`) for the glow's breathing and
   * each axis of the pupil's sweep. X and Y differ so the sweep traces a
   * Lissajous path rather than a degenerate straight line. */
  const PULSE_FREQ = 0.15
  const SWEEP_FREQ_X = 0.12
  const SWEEP_FREQ_Y = 0.19
  /** Pupil wander radius as a fraction of the iris radius; Y is scaled down
   * further so the scan reads as mostly side-to-side, like a search. */
  const PUPIL_SWEEP_RATIO = 0.45
  const PUPIL_SWEEP_Y_RATIO = 0.6
  const PUPIL_RADIUS_RATIO = 0.42
  const GLINT_ALPHA = 0.85

  const draw = (ctx, width, height, t) => {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height)

    const cx = width / 2
    const cy = height / 2
    const irisRadius = Math.min(width, height) * IRIS_RADIUS_RATIO
    if (irisRadius <= 0) return

    // Ambient glow: see the header's `globalAlpha` + `'transparent'`-stop
    // note — the far stop needs no relation to `irisColor`'s format, and the
    // pulse's peak brightness rides on `globalAlpha`, not a baked-in alpha.
    const pulse = GLOW_ALPHA_BASE + GLOW_ALPHA_AMPLITUDE * Math.sin(t * PULSE_FREQ * TAU)
    const glowRadius = irisRadius * GLOW_RADIUS_RATIO
    const glow = ctx.createRadialGradient(cx, cy, 0, cx, cy, glowRadius)
    glow.addColorStop(0, irisColor)
    glow.addColorStop(1, 'transparent')
    ctx.globalAlpha = pulse
    ctx.fillStyle = glow
    ctx.beginPath()
    ctx.arc(cx, cy, glowRadius, 0, TAU)
    ctx.fill()
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1

    // Iris: a flat solid disc — see the header for why this is not a
    // light-core/dark-rim gradient.
    ctx.fillStyle = irisColor
    ctx.beginPath()
    ctx.arc(cx, cy, irisRadius, 0, TAU)
    ctx.fill()

    // Pupil: a Lissajous sweep, pure `sin` with no phase offset on either
    // axis, so `t = 0` is always a centered gaze (see the header's reduced
    // motion note).
    const sweep = irisRadius * PUPIL_SWEEP_RATIO
    const px = cx + Math.sin(t * SWEEP_FREQ_X * TAU) * sweep
    const py = cy + Math.sin(t * SWEEP_FREQ_Y * TAU) * sweep * PUPIL_SWEEP_Y_RATIO
    const pupilRadius = irisRadius * PUPIL_RADIUS_RATIO
    ctx.fillStyle = pupilColorValue
    ctx.beginPath()
    ctx.arc(px, py, pupilRadius, 0, TAU)
    ctx.fill()

    // Glint: a small fixed highlight offset up-left from the pupil's center,
    // so the eye reads as glossy rather than a flat dot.
    ctx.globalAlpha = GLINT_ALPHA
    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff'
    ctx.beginPath()
    ctx.arc(px - pupilRadius * 0.35, py - pupilRadius * 0.35, pupilRadius * 0.3, 0, TAU)
    ctx.fill()
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1
  }

  const handleFrame = (frame) => {
    const t = frame.reducedMotion ? 0 : (frame.timestamp / 1000) * rateMultiplier
    draw(frame.ctx, frame.width, frame.height, t)
  }

  useCanvasSurface(() => hostEl, {
    onFrame: handleFrame,
  })
}

@template {
  <div
    ref={hostEl}
    data-slot="goldeneye"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class={cn('aihu-goldeneye')}></div>
}

@style {
  .aihu-goldeneye {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
}
